Weather Forecasts

Weather is particularly important for volume forecasting because consumption can have a strong relationship with weather.

For example:

Cold weather
     ↓
Higher heating demand
     ↓
Higher expected volume

or:

Warm weather
     ↓
Lower heating demand
     ↓
Different consumption profile

The architecture therefore brings Weather Forecasts into CDP staging.

3. Retail-related inputs

The architecture also contains several inputs specifically related to Retail forecasting.

Retail Commercial Forecasts

These provide commercial forecast information for the retail side.

Flow:

Retail Commercial Forecasts
            ↓
CDP
            ↓
User Corrections
            ↓
Retail Commercial Forecast
            ↓
Retail SMART Forecast
Retail Customer Segmentation

Customers can be grouped into different segments.

For example, conceptually:

Customer A → Domestic
Customer B → Commercial
Customer C → Industrial

Different customer segments behave differently, so segmentation is important to forecasting.

The architecture therefore has:

Retail Customer Segmentation
              ↓
Corrected / approved segmentation
              ↓
Forecast generation
4. Industry Meter Data

The diagram shows Industry Meter Data coming through an External Corrections process.

This is an important architectural distinction.

The diagram states that:

Industry Data is staged and corrected outside of the CDP system.

So not every correction happens inside VFS2/CDP.

The flow is effectively:

Industry Meter Data
        ↓
External Corrections
        ↓
Corrected Industry Data
        ↓
CDP / VFS2 processing

This means VFS2 has to consume data that may already have gone through an external correction process.
